Not in *anything* period.  Ctrl-C in Unix (where nethack gets its
pedigree) sends SIGINT which programs are encouraged to handle
intelligently.  See, for instance, sh, ed, and vi for 3 old and
traditional programs that don't get "stopped cold" by Ctrl-C (they all
interrupt _something_ but don't themselves die in response).  It's
actually not very common for curses programs (of which nethack is an
example) to die in response to ctrl-c; some do (e.g top) but many
don't (e.g. vi, emacs, pine, trn).



